    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d42">

                <pb facs="0146" n="40" sameAs="frus:frus1933-39/pg40"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.841 Russia/51</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Soviet Chargé</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Skvirsky</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                            State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1933-11-21">November 21, 1933</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your note of November 20,
                    1933, I wish to inform you that in accordance with the Soviet Statute on Port
                    Duties of February <pb facs="0147" n="41" xml:id="pg41"/> 19, 1926, there are
                    two categories of tonnage duties in ports of the U. S. S. R—ordinary and
                    preferential. The preferential duties are levied on ships of countries having
                    special agreements with the U. S. S. R. The People’s Commissariat for Water
                    Transport, by agreement with the Peopled Commissariat for Foreign Affairs and
                    the People’s Commissariat for Foreign Trade, may apply the preferential rate to
                    countries having normal diplomatic relations with the U. S. S. R. In accordance
                    with this statute the People’s Commissariat for Water Transport has issued Order
                    No. 427, effective this day, November 21, 1933, which reads as follows: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“On the basis of Article 2 of the Statute on Port Duties, a tonnage duty
                            of 10 kopeks per registered ton of net capacity is established, on a
                            reciprocal basis, for vessels flying the flag of the United States of
                            America.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>This duty of 10 kopeks constitutes the preferential rate. Thus beginning November
                    21, 1933, the vessels flying the flag of the United States of America have been
                    accorded the preferential rate of tonnage duty. It may be added that no
                    discriminating duties are levied in ports of the Soviet Union on produce,
                    manufactures or merchandise imported in American vessels.<anchor n="38"
                        xml:id="d42-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        B. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Skvirsky</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d42-f1" n="38" xml:id="d42-f1-content">
                <p>The Department, in its reply of January 29, 1934, informed the Soviet Ambassador
                    of the reciprocal proclamation signed January 10, 1934, effective as of November
                    21, 1933 (811.841 Russia/59); for text of proclamation, see 48 Stat. 1729.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
